leila (Docs): Drafted a new troubleshooting reference for 429s, timeouts, and context-limit hits. Included a short section on graceful fallbacks and a couple of SDK snippets. Can someone sanity-check the language and the snippet behavior? PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/498

raj (Eng): Quick skim looks good. A few notes: call out both Retry-After and X-RateLimit-Reset headers, and make clear that Retry-After may be seconds or HTTP-date. Also mention that spikes are often due to hot prefixes in batching.

amy (SDK): Agree. For SDK examples we should prefer the built-in retry utilities and not roll custom backoffs in user code. I added a tiny JS example that uses the SDK helper and then falls back to a smaller model when 'context_length_exceeded' is returned. Pasteable snippet for the PR:  

```js
// JS: use SDK retry helper + fallback to compact model on context errors
import { Redwood, RetryStrategy } from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });

const retry = new RetryStrategy({ maxAttempts: 4, baseMs: 200, capMs: 10000 });

async function fetchWithFallback(req) {
  try {
    return await client.generate(req, { retry });
  } catch (err) {
    const r = err?.response;
    if (r?.status === 429) {
      // respect Retry-After if present
      const ra = r.headers?.['retry-after'];
      if (ra) { /* show code reading and parsing here in docs */ }
    }
    if (err?.body?.error?.code === 'context_length_exceeded') {
      // immediate fallback: trim recent messages or switch model
      const trimmed = trimOldMessages(req.messages, 8000);
      return client.generate({ ...req, messages: trimmed, model: 'redwood-compact-v1' });
    }
    throw err;
  }
}
```

oscar (Support): From INC-2102 we saw two archetypes: sustained high QPS (needs reserved/dedicated) and transient bursts (retry + jitter). Add a short troubleshooting checklist: 1) check usage spikes in console, 2) inspect Retry-After/X-RateLimit headers, 3) trim system messages or compressed summaries, 4) consider dedicated capacity if this is production steady state.

leila (Docs): Good list. I'll add the checklist as a collapsible block and link to the Dedicated capacity page. Also will add a tiny stress repro command (stress-client.sh) for the examples repo so folks can reproduce a 429 locally for testing.

raj (Eng): One more micro-ask: under 'timeouts' clarify the difference between request-level timeouts and model-generation stalls (streaming vs non-streaming). Example: show how to set client-side timeout and how to enable streaming fallback.

amy (SDK): I'll add a Python example mirroring the JS pattern that uses our SDK retry decorator and the helper trim_tokens() function from examples. Suggest linking to examples repo util.

ci-bot: build/preview is ready for docs/pr-498 at https://preview.docs.redwood.ai/pr-498 — preview includes new troubleshooting page.

leila (Docs): Thanks everyone. I'll push updates (headers parsing note, checklist, stress script link, small paragraph on cost tradeoffs when switching models) and merge after CI. If support flags anything sensitive to remove, ping me and I'll sanitize.
